# ========================================================== swarm_copter.parm - additional parameters
# for multicopter SITL ============================================================================ Load
# chain: copter.parm, gazebo-iris.parm, THIS FILE (last).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- ENVIRONMENT CONTRACT (from companion_scripts/swarm_sitl.parm) --------- 6 vehicle + Gazebo on the
# same CPU: 400 Hz main loop does not catch up on laptop.
SCHED_LOOP_RATE 200
# The Gazebo Iris model has one compass; looking for a second/third blocks pre-arm.
COMPASS_USE2 0
COMPASS_PRIO2_ID 0
COMPASS_USE3 0
COMPASS_PRIO3_ID 0
COMPASS_TYPEMASK 2
# Relax the accelerometer inconsistency threshold for SITL.
INS_ACC_BODYFIX 2

# --- ROLL 7-10 Hz LIMIT CYCLE CORRECTION (measured 2026-08-08) ------ Model Gazebo iris_base is
# physically inconsistent: roll inertia ixx=0.008, pitch iyy=0.015 while the motor arms are
# asymmetrical in the REVERSE direction (y=+-0.22 roll arm, x=+-0.13 pitch arm). Plant gain of roll
# axis is ~3.2 times pitch; ArduCopter applies the SAME ATC_RAT_* gain to both. Result: roll is
# inherently marginally stable. When you add the phase delay SCHED_LOOP_RATE 200 (instead of 400), it
# enters the limit cycle. Measured (hover, ALT_HOLD, live SITL): D=0.0036 (stock) -> roll 15.87 dps
# RMS @7.5 Hz, angle 0.187 deg D=0.0010 -> roll 0.60 dps RMS, angle 0.010 deg (26x) Pitch is clear in
# both cases (0.2-0.5 dps) - axis evidence of its asymmetry. NOTE: This vibration existed since the
# first commit (9.4 Hz, 11.2 dps in 08-01 logs). Gimbal is NOT THE CAUSE; just added mass and lowered
# the frequency to 9.4->7.5 Hz. It wasn't there in the original ../guidance_value.sh because it didn't load
# parm at all, so SCHED_LOOP_RATE remained at the default 400.
ATC_RAT_RLL_D 0.001

# =====================================================================
# FLIGHT ENVELOPE USED IN THESE TRIALS
# Each [min..max] annotation gives the range in the ArduPilot parameter
# definition (libraries/AC_WPNav/AC_WPNav.cpp, AC_Loiter.cpp,
# AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.cpp and ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp).
# On 2026-08-05 the speed ceiling changed from 20 to 24 m/s to match the
# physical platform: formation_KILLER.py NAV_SPEED=24.0, WPNAV_SPEED=2400,
# ATTACK_SPEED_MPS=24.0. The metadata @Range recommendation of 2000 is not a
# hard limit; the physical vehicle accepted 2400 during flight.
# With a target cruising at 20 m/s, equal 20 m/s ceilings provided no closing
# speed. A 24 m/s ceiling provides a 20% speed margin.

# --- Horizontal speed / acceleration ---
WPNAV_SPEED 3500          # -> 35 m/s. Measured: setting 2400 produced 24.15 m/s,
                          # with only 18.5 deg bank (60 deg ceiling) and 0.1 m altitude loss. 35
                          # m/s was seen in real flight.
WPNAV_ACCEL 500           # [50..500]  cm/s2 -> 5 m/s2 (CEILING)
LOIT_SPEED 3500           # [20..3500] cm/s  - retain speed in modes other than GUIDED
LOIT_ACC_MAX 981          # [100..981] cm/s2 (CEILING)

# --- Vertical speed / acceleration ---
WPNAV_SPEED_UP 1000       # [10..1000] cm/s -> 10 m/s (CEILING)
WPNAV_SPEED_DN 500        # [10..500]  cm/s ->  5 m/s (CEILING)
WPNAV_ACCEL_Z 500         # [50..500]  cm/s2 (CEILING)
PILOT_SPEED_UP 1000
PILOT_SPEED_DN 500

# --- Jerk (how hard to react to speed commands) ---
PSC_JERK_XY 20            # [1..20]  m/s3 (CEILING)
PSC_JERK_Z 50             # [5..50]  m/s3 (CEILING)

# --- Tilt angle --- ANGLE_MAX range [1000..8000] cdeg. 8000 (80 degree) NOT WRITTEN: the thrust
# required to hold that tilt is 1/cos(80) = 5.8 x hover thrust, thrust/weight ratio of the iris SITL
# model ~2. Writing a limit that cannot be reached will cause the multicopter to lose altitude while
# trying to bank. cos(60) = 0.5 -> 2x, so 6000 is just above the PHYSICAL ceiling of the model; It is
# reduced if necessary after the measurement.
ANGLE_MAX 6000            # [1000..8000] cdeg -> 60 degrees

# --- Yaw (speed of turning nose to target) --- NO gimbal on HORIZONTAL axis (2026-08-05 gimbal
# branch: gimbal SINGLE AXIS, tilt/vertical only). So horizontal framing is still entirely dependent
# on the airframe yaw: the nose has to keep the bearing to keep the target in frame; 120 Bearing speed
# for m passes ~10 degrees/s. This reasoning applies EXACTLY to the yaw. NO LONGER VALID for VERTICAL
# axis: camera on self-stabilizing physical tilt gimbal; body pitch (measured: +-35 deg) is not
# reflected in the image (max |0.65| deg). The tilt command determines the vertical framing, not pitch
# (scripts/standoff_geom.sh -> YILDIZ_TILT). see GIMBAL_NOTES.md
ATC_SLEW_YAW 18000        # [500..18000] cdeg/s -> 180 degrees/s (CEILING)
ATC_ACCEL_Y_MAX 72000     # [0..72000] cdeg/s2 (CEILING)
